{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1788368854,"nonce":"P3REF89sKWMc6ESl8k90MPTKDcU=","target":"a8d8ada9794e947f6d0ebb6babbfa7d1fe767778388df5b0fc5db6cf2047cce2","message":"Observed live on the bench, 2026-09-02, while proving `eef837a`. A fresh `Corpse_Cat` was unforbidden, the butcher bill went `workable`, the run advanced ~80,000 ticks — and the corpse was **gone**. No `Meat_Cat`, no butcher job, bill back to `asleep-no-matching-ingredient`.\n\nA wild animal ate it. Ordinary RimWorld. From the protocol's side the thing ceased to exist between two calls with nothing anywhere saying so.\n\n**The ask, in Evan's words: \"I just wanted there to be a reason when a corpse randomly disappears.\"** That is the whole issue. Nothing more.\n\n\nNot on the thing. A destroyed corpse has no row — `things --def Corpse_Cat` returns `count: 0`, and any field describing its condition dies with it. Anything that only shows up *before* the disappearance answers a different question than the one being asked.\n\nIt has to be the journal:\n\n- The journal is history and survives the thing.\n- **The agent is forced to read it every turn** by `722c951`'s `unread-journal` guard, so the event is guaranteed to be seen.\n- `JournalHooks.cs` already Harmony-patches ten members and the journal already carries twelve event types. This is one more of an established kind.\n- **`Thing.Destroy(DestroyMode mode)` supplies the reason directly**: `Vanish`, `WillReplace`, `KillFinalize`, `KillFinalizeLeavingsOnly`, `Deconstruct`, `FailConstruction`, `Cancel`, `Refund`, `QuestLogic`. The game already says which kind of ending it was, so nothing has to be inferred.\n\n\n1. A journal event when a corpse is destroyed, carrying `def`, `at`, `id` and the `DestroyMode`. Cite the patched member.\n2. The `DestroyMode` is reported as the game's own value and **not re-interpreted into a story**. `Vanish` is `Vanish`, not \"eaten by a predator\".\n3. **Corpses only.** `Thing.Destroy` is extremely hot — every item, every stack merge — so it must not be patched globally. If the hook cannot be made cheap enough on a 38-mod bench, **park it and say so** rather than shipping a per-item patch. Widening beyond corpses is a separate decision with a stated reason.\n4. `checklists/turn.md`'s journal read gains one line: a destroy event for something you were planning around means it is gone — re-plan, do not re-query.\n5. Bench check as a markdown command list, not a Python suite: leave a fresh corpse where wildlife will take it, advance, assert the journal carries the event with a mode.\n\n\n- Any condition-of-the-corpse signal such as a missing-part fraction. A number like \"60% gone\" is a soft cue with no threshold behind it; it invites the agent to make a judgement call instead of reading a fact, and it does not answer the question that was asked.\n- Identifying what ate it, distinguishing fire from teeth, or reconstructing a history.\n\n\n`eef837a` killed three colonists because the agent could not see why a bill would not run. This is the same class one step out: the agent now gets a correct `no-matching-ingredient` verdict but cannot tell \"your ingredient was destroyed\" from \"the game is behaving strangely.\" The cost of the wrong conclusion is a run that stops trusting its own instruments.\n\nRelated: `f1a1700` (parked) is the room-and-building half of \"things vanish silently\", blocked on room identity. This is the thing half and is cheaper — a destroy hook needs no durable handle, only the moment.","files":null}]}